demand are you actually addressing? Would

1046
01:35:34.590 --> 01:35:39.394
the support reduce unnecessary effort, or
add another thing the student must

1047
01:35:39.395 --> 01:35:44.326
manage? Then consider how the student can
show or tell you whether it helps. A

1048
01:35:44.327 --> 01:35:49.387
resource can look organised to an adult
and still be confusing or burdensome for

1049
01:35:49.388 --> 01:35:54.320
its intended user. We’re looking for
better access and participation, with the

1050
01:35:54.321 --> 01:35:59.188
student’s experience helping us judge
usefulness. More compliance pressure is

1051
01:35:59.189 --> 01:35:59.957
not the aim.

1052
01:36:01.480 --> 01:36:06.536
Predictability means having accessible
information about routine and change. It

1053
01:36:06.537 --> 01:36:11.853
doesn’t mean every day must be identical,
or that we can promise nothing unexpected

1054
01:36:11.854 --> 01:36:16.392
will happen. Think about how much
information in a primary classroom is

1055
01:36:16.393 --> 01:36:20.931
spoken once, implied, or held in an
adult’s head. If I couldn’t rely on

1056
01:36:20.932 --> 01:36:26.118
remembering what someone had told me, how
would I know what was happening here? A

1057
01:36:26.119 --> 01:36:31.175
useful support helps students answer four
questions: what is happening, what is

1058
01:36:31.176 --> 01:36:36.363
changing, what comes next, and what can I
do if the plan changes? The information

1059
01:36:36.364 --> 01:36:40.901
needs to arrive early enough to be useful.
Imagine an assembly has been

1060
01:36:40.902 --> 01:36:45.894
moved earlier. How will students find out?
Has the change been made clear on a

1061
01:36:45.895 --> 01:36:51.016
schedule or another accessible support?
Can they return to that information, see

1062
01:36:51.017 --> 01:36:55.620
what follows, and ask a question or
request help in their preferred way?

1063
01:36:55.621 --> 01:37:00.353
Another spoken announcement may not answer
all those needs. Return to your

1064
01:37:00.354 --> 01:37:05.216
rhythm audit. When something in that
rhythm changes, how do students know? A

1065
01:37:05.217 --> 01:37:10.209
visual support is one option, but the
format needs to fit the people using it.

1066
01:37:10.210 --> 01:37:13.321
Review it through actual experience and
feedback.

1067
01:37:14.840 --> 01:37:19.626
A schedule can make sequence, timing and
change visible. It gives students

1068
01:37:19.627 --> 01:37:24.609
information they can return to, rather
than relying entirely on listening and

1069
01:37:24.610 --> 01:37:29.264
remembering. Schedules can cover different
spans: a first-then sequence,

1070
01:37:29.265 --> 01:37:34.182
one activity, part of the day or the whole
day. Choose a format the intended

1071
01:37:34.183 --> 01:37:39.362
student can access and understand. The
question is whether it shows their actual

1072
01:37:39.363 --> 01:37:44.214
day, including choices, pauses and
changes, or only the tasks adults expect

1073
01:37:44.215 --> 01:37:48.935
them to complete. Make flexibility visible
too. If something changes, how

1074
01:37:48.936 --> 01:37:53.787
does the schedule show that? Does it still
provide useful information after

1075
01:37:53.788 --> 01:37:58.574
the original plan has changed? Can the
student see what comes next without

1076
01:37:58.575 --> 01:38:03.229
needing another spoken explanation? A
schedule is useful when it reduces

1077
01:38:03.230 --> 01:38:08.343
uncertainty and supports participation for
the person using it. Its presence on

1078
01:38:08.344 --> 01:38:13.392
the wall or desk isn’t enough. Watch it in
use, invite feedback, and adjust it

1079
01:38:13.393 --> 01:38:16.605
when it no longer answers the student’s
questions.

1080
01:38:18.120 --> 01:38:22.902
With any visual support, start by asking
what the student needs to find,

1081
01:38:22.903 --> 01:38:27.819
understand, remember or communicate. Then
choose a form that can make that

1082
01:38:27.820 --> 01:38:32.804
information clear. It might be an object,
photograph, symbol, written word,

1083
01:38:32.805 --> 01:38:38.125
checklist, map or choice support. Visual
does not automatically mean accessible.

1084
01:38:38.126 --> 01:38:43.379
The student needs to understand what it
represents and be able to use it at the

1085
01:38:43.380 --> 01:38:48.633
point of need. Keep unnecessary detail
out, explain how it works, and check its

1086
01:38:48.634 --> 01:38:53.820
meaning with the student. Page nineteen
gives four quick-review questions. Can

1087
01:38:53.821 --> 01:38:58.603
the student understand it? Can they access
it when they need it? Does it

1088
01:38:58.604 --> 01:39:03.453
reflect what is actually happening,
including change? Does it give useful

1089
01:39:03.454 --> 01:39:08.572
information or a way to respond? Keep
understanding and expressing in view. A

1090
01:39:08.573 --> 01:39:13.355
visual support may help someone
understand, while A A C provides ways to

1091
01:39:13.356 --> 01:39:18.744
communicate. Access to A A C doesn’t mean
every spoken instruction or schedule is

1092
01:39:18.745 --> 01:39:23.257
understood. Keep the student’s
communication system available during

1093
01:39:23.258 --> 01:39:28.376
routines, transitions and changes, and
recognise responses beyond speech. The

1094
01:39:28.377 --> 01:39:33.428
Visual Supports Checklist and the online
webinar offer further learning. The

1095
01:39:33.429 --> 01:39:38.413
checklist is a set of questions for
personalisation and consultation, not a

1096
01:39:38.414 --> 01:39:43.869
prescription. Keep asking: does this give
this student useful information they can

1097
01:39:43.870 --> 01:39:45.890
understand, access and act on?

1098
01:39:47.400 --> 01:39:52.081
Return to your brief and the option cards.
Which one or two options answer

1099
01:39:52.082 --> 01:39:56.505
it? You’ve heard about executive
functioning supports, predictability,

1100
01:39:56.506 --> 01:40:01.700
schedules and visual supports. Sort those
possibilities against the demand in your

1101
01:40:01.701 --> 01:40:06.510
routine. Choose one or two. For each,
write down why it fits: which specific

1102
01:40:06.511 --> 01:40:11.576
demand does it answer? Record the choice
and your reason on section two of the A

1103
01:40:11.577 --> 01:40:16.514
three sheet. You can also follow the
prompts on page eighteen. Give yourselves

1104
01:40:16.515 --> 01:40:21.516
about three minutes. If everything seems
useful, return to the brief and narrow

1105
01:40:21.517 --> 01:40:26.262
the choice. We’re making a manageable
response to a known demand. Pause the

1106
01:40:26.263 --> 01:40:27.801
video for your decision.

1107
01:40:35.840 --> 01:40:40.804
Let’s come back. Check that you have a
reason beside each option. Saying that a

1108
01:40:40.805 --> 01:40:45.704
support is often helpful is different from
saying what it will make clearer or

1109
01:40:45.705 --> 01:40:50.096
easier in this routine. Keep that
connection visible. It will help you

1110
01:40:50.097 --> 01:40:54.615
explain the design, decide what to
observe, and revise the support if it

1111
01:40:54.616 --> 01:40:56.970
doesn’t help in the way you expected.

1112
01:40:58.480 --> 01:41:03.700
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

1113
01:41:03.701 --> 01:41:08.858
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

1114
01:41:08.859 --> 01:41:13.633
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

1115
01:41:13.634 --> 01:41:18.663
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

1116
01:41:18.664 --> 01:41:23.566
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

1117
01:41:23.567 --> 01:41:28.405
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

1118
01:41:28.406 --> 01:41:33.180
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

1119
01:41:33.181 --> 01:41:37.956
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

1120
01:41:37.957 --> 01:41:42.859
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
